<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kublai Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1215">1215</a>–<a href="/years/1294">1294</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kublai Khan was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> in 1279 and ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>. His disputed succession contributed to the fragmentation of the Mongol Empire, and his expeditions overseas, including against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, influenced the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kublai-khan.jpg" alt="Kublai Khan" width="320" height="399" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Araniko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumKhubilaiPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>That changed when Alghu (r. 1260–1266) used the civil war between <b>Kublai</b> and Ariq Böke to assert real independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chagatai-empire">Chagatai Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese histories of the 7th century counted it the chief tributary state of the South, on a par with Koguryŏ and Baekje, and <b>Kublai Khan</b> ordered a mobile secretariat set up there to dominate the trade of the South China Sea and the Indian Ocean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/champa">Champa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Majapahit's founder Raden Wijaya used a Mongol army sent by <b>Kublai Khan</b> to defeat his rival, then turned on the invaders and drove them from Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After the succession war between <b>Kublai</b> and Ariq Böke (1260–1264), the Golden Horde, Ilkhanate, Chagatai Khanate and Yuan dynasty operated as largely independent states, though they continued to acknowledge a shared Chinggisid heritage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>That changed when the Yuan dynasty of <b>Kublai Khan</b> demanded submission in the 1270s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>War with the Mongols began in 1235 under the Great Khan Ögedei and continued under Möngke, who died while campaigning in Sichuan in 1259, and finally <b>Kublai Khan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Died in Khanbaliq, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1266" aria-label="Khanbaliq on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7417 735 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Kublai Khan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7867 1005h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7867 1005h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=39.93,116.4&amp;year=1266">What was here in 1266? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I worship and pay respect to all four, and pray that he among them who is greatest in heaven in very truth may aid me.&#8221;</p><footer>On the festivals of the Christians, Saracens, Jews and Buddhists (Marco Polo, Travels, book 2, Ramusio's text) &#183; tr. Henry Yule, 1871 (3rd ed., rev. Henri Cordier, 1903) &#183; Marco Polo's account; &quot;all four&quot; are the prophets of those faiths: Jesus, Muhammad, Moses and the Buddha &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10636/pg10636-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…and thus in the end there will be more Christians here than exist in your part of the world!&#8221;</p><footer>To the Polo brothers, asking the Pope for a hundred learned men (Marco Polo, Travels, book 2, Ramusio's text) &#183; tr. Henry Yule, 1871 (3rd ed., rev. Henri Cordier, 1903) &#183; Marco Polo's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10636/pg10636-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
